w separates rigid silver from the strands. The barrette, cloth wrap and braid terminal are neighboring parts of the same visible root assembly, while their separate materials remain readable. Rain beads cling to the glass panes; a shallow catch tray and recovered, slightly wavy herbarium papers on the bench make the recent leak and the rescue work understandable. Green leaves beyond the glass and the worn wooden press surround her without competing with the hair structure. Clear late-afternoon light after rain reveals strand crossings, cloth folds, silver edges and naturally textured skin. Photograph the scene from her front-right, about 65 degrees around toward her right side and 18 degrees above eye level. Keep environmental portrait framing, her whole head through below her waist, the entire tail and both working hands, with enough depth of field for the right temple, ear-side braid, fastening root and press to remain readable. The photograph carries a quiet, warm pause in purposeful work.",
